- a supplier that has a place of business in Canada where it conducts activities on a permanent basis which is clearly identified by name and accessible during normal business hours; or, a joint venture where each member of the joint venture has a place of business in Canada where it conducts activities on a permanent basis that is clearly identified by name and accessible during normal business hours; and
- is registered and files taxes in Canada (e.g., GST / HST , corporate income tax);
- maintains a registered address in Canada and employs personnel and/or conducts day-to-day business activities in Canada; and will not subcontract work to non-Canadian suppliers or individuals located outside Canada, in a manner that results in minimal value-added activities being performed within Canada.

- The Offeror acknowledges that the contracting authority may, based on supporting evidence and consideration of all relevant facts and circumstances, determine that the Offeror does not meet the definition of a Canadian supplier for the purposes of the Policy.
- The Offeror acknowledges that the discovery of false or misleading information, or failure to provide requested evidence, may result in contractual remedies including, but not limited to:
- Offer disqualification;
- Liquidated damages;
- Ineligibility for contract award;
- Retention of deposits or holdbacks;
- Termination of contract, setting aside of standing offers or suspension or cancellation of supply arrangements;
- Disqualification under the Ineligibility and Suspension Policy .

- The offeror attests that they will remain in full compliance with the statements made in this attestation throughout the offer period and any resulting contract.
- The offeror attests that evidence regarding the origin of goods and/or services is available and agrees to provide any supporting documentation requested for verification purposes, at any time, including during reviews, in response to complaints, or after contract award.
- The offeror acknowledges that the contracting authority may, based on supporting evidence and consideration of all relevant facts and circumstances, determine that the goods or services do not meet the definition of Canadian value-added for the purposes of the Policy.
- The offeror acknowledges that the discovery of false or misleading information, or failure to provide requested evidence, may result in contractual remedies including, but not limited to:
- Offer disqualification;
- Liquidated damages;
- Ineligibility for contract award;
- Retention of deposits or holdbacks;
- Termination of contract or setting aside of standing offers, or suspension or cancellation of supply arrangements;
- Disqualification under the Ineligibility and Suspension Policy.
